<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the performance of Chimera-Seg on two datasets: COCO-Stuff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and PASCAL Context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>. Note that the split of seen and unseen classes follows the settings of the previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>. Note that the ‘background’ is converted to ‘ignored’ in both training and inference. This dataset is split into 15 seen and 5 unseen classes. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">COCO-Stuff</span> contains 118,287 training and 5,000 validation images. For ZSS, all 171 classes are grouped into 156 seen and 15 unseen classes. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PASCAL Context</span> maintains 4,996 training images and 5,104 testing images. In our experiment, the classes consist of 49 seen classes and 10 unseen classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implementation Details</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method is based on MMSegmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> and trained on 8 V100 GPUs with 32GB memories per card. Unless specified, we use Segformer-B0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> for most of the ablation studies. The visual encoder we use in CLIP is ViT-B/16 and the text encoder is the corresponding text encoder. For the hyperparameters, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is set as 0.07, the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in SGD is from 9000 and decreased 0.1 as each iteration, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is set as 0.01, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is set as 0.07. The batch size we use is 16 and the model is trained in 40K and 80K iterations for PASCAL Context, and COCO-Stuff, respectively. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{sam}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{sam}</annotation></semantics></math> are set as 0.1. Input images are resized to 512 <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 512. Other hyperparameters follow the settings of CLIP2Seg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and the corresponding backbones. During inference in the COCO-Stuff dataset, we add a constant number <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to the logits of unseen classes, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For ZSS, not only the seen classes but also the unseen classes should be considered. Therefore, following other researches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we apply hIoU as the metric:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hIoU=\frac{2\cdot sIoU\cdot uIoU}{sIoU+uIoU}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hIoU=\frac{2\cdot sIoU\cdot uIoU}{sIoU+uIoU},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sIoU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sIoU</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the mIoU (mean Intersetcion-over-Union) for the seen classes and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uIoU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uIoU</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the mIoU for the unseen classes. We follow the zero-shot semantic segmentation (ZSS) setting, where annotations of unseen classes are removed from the training set while retaining all input images. We consider both inductive and transductive settings based on the availability of unseen class embeddings (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>) during training. In the inductive setting, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is not accessible, and unseen regions are treated as ‘ignored’. The transductive setting uses <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for training. Evaluation covers both seen and unseen classes.</p>
</div>
